    filedata = file.read()

# use regular expressions to find assertions
assertions = re.findall(r'// @@@.*', filedata)

# print only the needed ones
for assertion in assertions:
    if 'It exists' in assertion:
        print(assertion)
```

This script doesn't generate the actual code. For that, you would need to parse the Python code you've posted, modify the required code snippets, and integrate them into the larger program.--------------------To generate output assert statements based on the comments that start with "@@@", you will need to use regular expressions in C# to parse the source code and identify the specific conditions specified in the comments. The below solution covers the last condition "If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in new list != old list count". 

Please see this C# code to generate the desired output,

```cs
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string sourceCode = // Your source code string goes here.
        
        // Find lines that contains the condition.
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(sourceCode, @"\/\/\s+@@@\s+If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in new list != old list count", RegexOptions.Multiline);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            
            // Split this line by 'LastIndexOf' for getting the variable names. Also '!' with inequality is removed for C# assert statement.
            string[] variables = match.Value.Split('║Þ','║Æ');
            string oldVariable = Regex.Replace(variables[1], @"last index of x in ", "old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x");
            string newVariable = Regex.Replace(variables[2], @"last index of x in ", "New_arrList_LastIndexOf_x");
            
            // Generate the assert statement. In C#, you can't specify an actual number for the last index in the list, so we will use the variable names.
            string assertStatement = "Debug.Assert(" + oldVariable + " != " + newVariable + ");";
            
            Console.WriteLine(assertStatement);
        }
    }
}
```

