The first debug.assert statement verifies that the count of the list before the operation is non-negative.

The second debug.assert checks if it is an add operation or a remove operation, by checking if the new count is equal to the old count + 1 or equal to the old count.

The third `Debug.Assert(Old_x == New_Ret);` checks if the returned value from `Add(x)` is equal to `x`.

The fourth line through the last one validates if the `IndexOf(x)` after the operation is valid, or if it does it mean that the item was not present before the operation.

Please note that the exact outputs might differ based on the actual structure of `arrList` for `Add(x)`, `Remove(x)` and `Insert(index,x)` methods as these are pseudo contracts for the `ArrayList` methods.

For the actual implementation of the `ArrayList`, consider the following details:
`ArrayList` might have to add an index to the actual items, then the `Get`, `Set`, `IndexOf`, `Contains`, `LastIndexOf` methods work differently, depending on the internal implementation. In addition, `LastIndexOf` also could not behave as expected, considering to the insertion order of items.

For the `CountContract`, it could fail according to the actual implementation of `ArrayList`. For the `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` contracts, they also could fail while using an internal data structure different than an array or list, as `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` may also depend on the content order, and how it has been implemented into `ArrayList` internally. Finally, the `Contains` contract will depend on the internal implementation of `HashTable` in case of it being used internally as it is based on hashing, and will depend on `List` implementation if a simple `List` is being used because it will need to check each entry one by one.--------------------Below is a csharp code using `Debug.Assert\(.*?\);`. 
